<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Release posture: no cleartext, anywhere, ever — and the three production hosts are pinned to the
  Let's Encrypt roots.

  Android's default already forbids cleartext at targetSdk 28+, but stating it explicitly is what
  makes it a decision rather than an inherited default — and what keeps a future library that
  quietly opts itself back in from succeeding (security concept §4).

  The dev flavour overrides this file to allow cleartext to the emulator's host loopback alone; a
  release build cannot inherit that, because the override lives in the dev source set. The dev
  override also carries NO pins, deliberately: the test stack's certificate is a throwaway signed
  by a CA that no longer exists, and pinning it would tie a debug build to a file in another repo.

  ── Why the CA and not the leaf ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Security concept §5 names the leaf-plus-backup-pin pattern as the stricter option and the CA pin
  as the one to evaluate first. This is that evaluation, and the CA pin wins on the property that
  actually matters here: it cannot brick the app.

  A leaf pin has to be rotated in an APK BEFORE the server rotates its key, and Let's Encrypt
  renews every sixty days with a fresh keypair unless the CSR is deliberately reused. That makes
  every renewal a release deadline, on a distribution channel (GitHub Releases plus Obtainium)
  where nothing forces a member to update. The first missed deadline takes the app away from
  everyone who has not updated, with no way to reach them — and OkHttp's own documentation says it
  plainly: "Certificate Pinning is Dangerous!".

  A root pin survives every renewal, needs no keypair reuse, and still excludes every other CA in
  the device's trust store — which is the attack it is there for: a mis-issued or coerced
  certificate from some other public CA. What it does NOT protect against is a mis-issuance by
  Let's Encrypt itself, and that is the honest limit of this choice rather than a gap to paper
  over.

  BOTH roots are pinned, and the second one is not decoration. ISRG Root X2 is the ECDSA root;
  Let's Encrypt has been issuing from its intermediates since 2024, and a certificate that chains
  to X2 with only X1 pinned fails to validate. Pinning one root would turn an ordinary CA-side
  change into an outage.

  ── The pins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Computed from the JDK trust store's own copies of the two roots rather than transcribed from a
  web page: a pin typed from memory is exactly how an app gets bricked, and these two values are
  the ones the rotation runbook tells the owner to recompute before touching this file.

    C5+lpZ7tcVwmwQIMcRtPbsQtWLABXhQzejna0wHFr8M=   ISRG Root X1 (RSA)
    diGVwiVYbubAI3RW4hB9xU8e/CH2GnkuvVFZE8zmgzI=   ISRG Root X2 (ECDSA)

  `expiration` is set on every pin-set. Android stops enforcing an expired pin-set rather than
  failing the connection, which is the one safety valve this design has: if both roots were ever
  replaced and nobody shipped an update, the app degrades to ordinary system trust instead of going
  dark. The date is deliberately inside X1's own validity (2035) — it is a deadline for us, not for
  the certificate.

  Rotation runbook: docs/ANDROID_APP_SECURITY.md §5.1.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <!-- The API host the app reads and writes through. -->
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">api.profit-base.online</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2028-08-24">
            <pin digest="SHA-256">C5+lpZ7tcVwmwQIMcRtPbsQtWLABXhQzejna0wHFr8M=</pin>
            <pin digest="SHA-256">diGVwiVYbubAI3RW4hB9xU8e/CH2GnkuvVFZE8zmgzI=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Keycloak: the token endpoint, the JWKS and the login the Custom Tab opens. -->
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">keycloak.profit-base.online</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2028-08-24">
            <pin digest="SHA-256">C5+lpZ7tcVwmwQIMcRtPbsQtWLABXhQzejna0wHFr8M=</pin>
            <pin digest="SHA-256">diGVwiVYbubAI3RW4hB9xU8e/CH2GnkuvVFZE8zmgzI=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>

    <!--
      The web frontend, which the app opens in a Custom Tab for the three public documents.

      Pinned for completeness, and with one limit written down: a Custom Tab runs in the browser's
      process, so this pin-set governs the app's own connections to the host and not the tab's.
      Chrome does its own validation there.
    -->
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">profit-base.online</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2028-08-24">
            <pin digest="SHA-256">C5+lpZ7tcVwmwQIMcRtPbsQtWLABXhQzejna0wHFr8M=</pin>
            <pin digest="SHA-256">diGVwiVYbubAI3RW4hB9xU8e/CH2GnkuvVFZE8zmgzI=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
